WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:06.581
Fantastic Beasts: The Crimes of Grindelwald
takes us back to 1927 and finds Newt Scamander

00:00:06.581 --> 00:00:09.360
caught up right in the thick of the dark wizard's
mess.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:14.009
So how do all the moving parts come together
in the latest Harry Potter prequel?

00:00:14.009 --> 00:00:17.480
Let's take a look at what happened at the
tail end of The Crimes of Grindelwald.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:23.200
This movie finally introduces us to Leta LeStrange,
Newt's old friend from Hogwarts who's now

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:25.160
engaged to his brother, Theseus.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:29.980
In the LeStrange family tomb, Leta reveals
that she swapped her real brother Corvus out

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:34.800
for a less colicky baby - after which the
ship they were on sank and her brother drowned.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:39.700
The boy who lived was Credence, and he was
given up for adoption after it was revealed

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:41.579
that he didn't belong to the LeStranges.

00:00:41.579 --> 00:00:46.789
She does, however, have another living brother
- Yusuf Kamas, who's on a mission to kill

00:00:46.789 --> 00:00:47.280
Corvus.

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:51.760
When Leta sacrifices herself to allow all
her new friends to escape Grindelwald's hall

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:55.380
of blue flames, the other characters might
have thought she could have been the last

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:57.440
of the LeStrange's pure blood lineage.

00:00:57.649 --> 00:01:02.320
Of course, Harry Potter fans know that's not
the case, since Bellatrix LeStrange is such

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:05.550
a big part of the Boy Wizard's journey many
years later.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:10.040
We'll have to wait to see whether and how
this loose thread ties up in later installments.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:14.940
Another subplot of the film concerns Newt
Scamander's reluctance to get involved in

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:17.170
the Aurors' effort to fight Grindelwald.

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:21.210
He's asked by the British Ministry of Magic
to join forces with them in exchange for the

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:27.009
restoration of his international travel rights,
but he refuses the offer, citing his disinterest

00:01:27.009 --> 00:01:30.000
in choosing sides in whatever battle they're
a part of.

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:34.520
But once he and Tina bear witness to what
Grindelwald wants to do and is capable of

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:38.720
doing, he realizes that this is a fight in
which no one can remain neutral.

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:43.200
After the loss of Leta, Newt tells Theseus
that he is now ready to choose and fight for

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:46.080
the side of the Ministry against this encroaching
threat.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:51.079
The history of Albus Dumbledore and Gellert
Grindelwald isn't fully explored in The Crimes

00:01:51.079 --> 00:01:54.860
of Grindelwald, but there are a few hints
that the two were more than just best friends

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:59.330
during their younger years - it's heavily
implied they were actually lovers who cared

00:01:59.330 --> 00:02:03.560
very much for each other until their interests
became so starkly divided.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:07.720
Before their split, the two made a decision
that they would never fight each other, and

00:02:07.729 --> 00:02:12.500
that promise was sealed by a blood oath that
has been cemented in a bauble worn by Grindelwald

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:13.340
at all times.

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:17.960
Even if that oath didn't stay Dumbledore's
hand, he still seems uneasy about going to

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:19.280
war with Grindelwald.

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:23.980
His affection appears to have survived whatever
caused their big split… at least for a time.

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:28.640
But once he retrieves the blood oath pendant
and sees a way he can possibly break the pact,

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:33.180
Dumbledore's chains to Grindelwald may soon
be broken, and he'd be able to fight his old

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:33.680
friend.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:38.840
Throughout The Crimes of Grindelwald, Credence
and Nagini are on the hunt for his real mother.

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:43.280
Once he finds the woman he's suspected to
be his mother, he's disappointed to discover

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:47.700
that she's actually just the family's servant,
and she's immediately killed off by a stray

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:49.800
spell from the Auror Grimmson.

00:02:49.910 --> 00:02:54.640
Credence is later lured to the LeStrange family
tomb, where he again hits a dead-end in his

00:02:54.650 --> 00:02:59.210
search, but after hearing Grindelwald's speech
about how wizards need to take over the world,

00:02:59.210 --> 00:03:01.960
he's ready to declare his allegiance to Team
Grindelwald.

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:06.840
Grindelwald reveals that Credence's real name
is Aurelius Dumbledore and says that Albus

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:08.600
Dumbledore is out to destroy him.

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:14.010
The implication is that he is an as-yet-unrevealed
third sibling or other relation to the Hogwarts

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:18.290
headmaster, which has left many Harry Potter
fans scratching their heads about how that

00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:23.270
timeline might have worked out - or if this
is just another bit of trickery by Grindelwald

00:03:23.270 --> 00:03:24.860
to make Credence resent Albus.

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:29.660
It's clear from the start of Fantastic Beasts:
The Crimes of Grindelwald that the title villain

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:30.920
is one clever wizard.

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:36.530
He manages to escape the clutches of his captors
and flits away to Paris, where he starts amassing

00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:41.700
an army of wizards by promising them untold
power and dominion over the known world.

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:44.520
"Do you think you can hold me?"

00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:49.700
Meanwhile, all of his talk about the wizards'
right to global superiority is immediately

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:52.400
reminiscent of the Axis powers in World War
II.

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:56.630
If the reference wasn't already clear enough,
though, the film crystallizes the connection

00:03:56.630 --> 00:04:01.510
by showing scenes of the exact conflict such
ideology led to, with visions of the city

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:06.590
being destroyed by air raids, an atomic bomb
being detonated in Japan, and even a glimpse

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:11.380
of Holocaust victims being marched to the
camps - scenes which, ironically, Grindelwald

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:14.820
says his planned takeover of the world will
specifically prevent.

00:04:15.020 --> 00:04:19.620
Regardless of whether those images were necessary,
however, the film made its point by declaring

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:23.300
this war to be a battle between the very ideas
of good and evil.

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:27.560
Of all the animals that are introduced in
Fantastic Beasts: The Crimes of Grindelwald,

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:29.510
perhaps the phoenix is the most significant.

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:34.500
To supplement Grindelwald's claim that Credence
is a member of the Dumbledore family, he shows

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:39.100
him how a baby bird becomes the fiery creature,
saying that the animals are known to come

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:42.240
to any member of the Dumbledore lineage in
their hour of need.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:46.360
His words are reminiscent of something Albus
Dumbledore had said about his own phoenix,

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:48.140
Fawkes, in the Harry Potter series.

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:52.720
It's unclear whether this new bird will bear
any relation to the bird that Dumbledore kept

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:56.950
in his office for all those years, but if
it does, the creature may end up being one

00:04:56.950 --> 00:05:01.160
of the few characters who helps bridge the
gap between the stories of Harry Potter and

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:02.210
Fantastic Beasts.

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:03.200
We'll see.

